      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to apologize.' Chef Barinov chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'wanted' for the ongoing action, followed by 'to apologise'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куча проблем из-за меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A lot of problems because of me.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'pile' directly with 'problems', combining 'pile' with 'problems'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отвезите меня по важному делу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Drive me for an important matter.' Chef Viktor uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'take'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я пришлю адрес.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will send the address.' Chef Barinov uses the perfective verb meaning 'will send' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'address'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Проспорил пять тысяч?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You lost the bet. Five thousand?' Fedya turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'lose a bet', pairing it with 'five' and 'thousand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Чего?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What?' Senya asks this with a single word meaning 'what?', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Мы спорили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We bet.' Fedya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to argue'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Может ли человек съесть ремень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If a person could eat a belt.' Fedya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'can' for the ongoing action, followed by 'a man' and 'to eat'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'yes' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты доказал, что может.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You proved they could.' Fedya uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'can', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Гони деньги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
